over the atomic contribution of each atom:
\[
E^{S R}=\sum_{i=1}^{N} E^{S R, i}=\sum_{i=1}^{N} f_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{S R}}\left(\boldsymbol{D}_{i}\right),
\]
where \(\boldsymbol{D}_{i}\) is the SR descriptor, comprising invariant features of the \(i\)-th atom. The neural network \(f_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{S R}}(\cdot)\), parameterized by \(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{S R}\), maps \(\boldsymbol{D}_{i}\) to the atomic SR energy \(E^{S R, i}\). The descriptor \(\boldsymbol{D}_{i}\) is a function of the local atomic environment \(\mathcal{R}_{i} \in \mathbb{R}^{\mathcal{N}_{i} \times 3}\), where \(\mathcal{N}_{i}\) represents the number of neighbors of atom \(i\) within a predefined cutoff \(r_{c}\). To ensure physical consistency, \(\boldsymbol{D}_{i}\) preserves symmetries such as translation, rotation and permutation. Further details on its practical construction are provided in Section S2 of the Supplementary Material (SM) [29].

Leveraging the rapid decay of LR interactions in Fourier space, we model the LR energy using multiple Fourier convolution layers:
\[
E^{L R}=\frac{1}{2 V} \sum_{i=1}^{N} \sum_{\eta=1}^{P} q_{i, \eta} \sum_{\boldsymbol{k}} \widehat{g}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}}(\boldsymbol{k}) \widehat{\rho}_{\eta}(\boldsymbol{k}) e^{i \boldsymbol{k} \cdot \boldsymbol{r}_{i}},
\]
where \(P \in \mathbb{Z}^{+}\)is the total number of layers,
\[
\boldsymbol{q}_{\eta}=\left(q_{1, \eta}, \cdots, q_{N, \eta}\right), \quad \eta=1, \cdots, P
\]
denotes the \(\eta\)-th layer of latent variables for the LR energy, \(\widehat{g}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}}\) is the SOG multiplier operator parametrized by \(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}\) for the \(\eta\)-th layer, \(\boldsymbol{k}=2 \pi\left(m_{x} / L_{x}, m_{y} / L_{y}, m_{z} / L_{z}\right)\) denotes the Fourier modes with \(m_{x}, m_{y}, m_{z} \in \mathbb{Z}\), and
\[
\widehat{\rho}_{\eta}(\boldsymbol{k})=\sum_{j=1}^{N} q_{j, \eta} e^{-i \boldsymbol{k} \cdot \boldsymbol{r}_{j}}
\]
denotes the structure factor of the latent variables for the \(\eta\)-th layer. In the LR model, the latent variables \(\boldsymbol{q}_{\eta}\) in each layer can be interpreted as the weights assigned to each atom for different types of LR interactions, such
as partial charges in electrostatic interactions or atomic sizes in dispersion interactions. These latent variables are obtained by mapping the invariant features \(\boldsymbol{D}_{i}\) to \(\boldsymbol{q}_{\eta}\) using a latent neural network parameterized by \(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{l n n}\), i.e., \(\left\{q_{i, \eta}\right\}_{\eta=1}^{P}=f_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text {lnn }}}\left(\boldsymbol{D}_{i}\right)\). A schematic diagram of the basic network structure is presented in the upper half of Figure 1.

In our LR model, two key aspects are the parameterization of the SOG multiplier operator \(\widehat{g}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}}\) and the efficiency in computing Eq. (2). These factors will be addressed sequentially as follows. We parameterize \(\hat{\boldsymbol{g}}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}}\) as a sum of Gaussians,
\[
\widehat{g}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}}(\boldsymbol{k})=\sum_{\ell=1}^{M} w_{\ell, \eta} e^{-k^{2} / s_{\ell, \eta}^{2}},
\]
where \(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}=\left\{w_{\ell, \eta}, s_{\ell, \eta}\right\}_{\ell=1}^{M}\) is the set of trainable parameters. The SOG multipliers \(\left\{\widehat{g}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}}\right\}_{\eta=1}^{P}\) serve as a multilayer approximation of the LR tail of the potential in Fourier space. Gaussians exhibit excellent symmetry and smoothness and are known to minimize the Heisenberg uncertainty principle for \(L^{2}\) functions 30 . These properties allow SOG multipliers to achieve an optimal trade-off between spatial and frequency localization, facilitating the effective representation of LR tails. The form of LR energy, as constructed in Eqs. (2)(5), inherently preserves translation, rotation, and permutation invariants. Additional constraints can be imposed either on the coefficients \(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}\) or by projecting the learned potential onto a set of symmetric basis functions [19, 31. In practice, the bandwidths are initialized as \(s_{\ell, \eta}=\exp \left[b_{\text {min }}+\ell\left(b_{\text {max }}-b_{\text {min }}\right) / M\right]\) with a uniform weight \(w_{\ell, \eta}=1\). The logarithmically spacing bandwidths effectively capture the multiscale nature of LR potentials and significantly reduce the number of Gaussians. Furthermore, after completing the training, \(M\) is further reduced using model reduction techniques 32 to improve the prediction efficiency. Moreover, we propose a fast algorithm embedded in the SOG-Net such that nearoptimal complexity and high scalability can be achieved; See Appendix A.

To validate the SOG-Net model, we start by investigating a toy model of NaCl electrolytes. This system consists of 1000 particles interacting through Coulomb and Lennard-Jones forces. The training set contains at most 4000 configurations, and the test set includes 200 configurations, both sampled every 1000 steps from a long MD trajectory using LAMMPS 33 in the NVT ensemble at \(T=300 \mathrm{~K}\). Further details on the training setup for the SR component are provided in Section S4 of 29 . For the LR component, we use a single layer of latent variables ( \(P=1\) ) and six Gaussians ( \(M=6\) ) in the LR convolution layer.

In Figure 2(a), we plot the test error as a function of the training set size, keeping the number of Fourier grids fixed as \(N_{\mathrm{FFT}}=21^{3}\). One observes that using the SR part alone results in limitations in the representational capacity. However, combining the two com-

