\section*{Appendix A: Fast algorithm in the SOG-Net}

Electrostatics is the bottleneck of MD with classical force fields. Many fast methods have been proposed for modeling and accelerating long-range electrostatics in the literature, including particle-mesh Ewald methods [53, 54], fast multipole methods [55, 56, random batch methods [57, real-space truncation approaches 58, 59, and reaction-field models 60. These techniques can in principle be introduced to speed up the machinelearned long-range force fields. The SOG is an efficient kernel approximation method that describes diverse longrange decay behaviors well. It also allows the design of fast algorithms to achieve close-to-linear computational complexity considering that the Fourier transform of a Gaussian remains a Gaussian. Figure 1 presents the schematic of the fast method embedded in the long-range network. It begins by mapping each layer of latent variables, \(q_{i, \eta}\), onto a uniform grid through convolution with
a shape function \(W\), referred to as the gridding operator \(\mathcal{G}_{\eta}\). The FFT operator \(\mathcal{F}\) is then applied to compute \(\widehat{\rho}_{\eta}(\boldsymbol{k})\). For each layer, the diagonal scaling operator \(\mathcal{S}_{\eta}\) performs pointwise multiplication \(\left(\widehat{g}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}} \circ \widehat{\rho}_{\eta}\right)(\boldsymbol{k})\) in the Fourier space. Subsequently, the inverse FFT operator \(\mathcal{F}^{-1}\) recovers the atomic LR energy at each grid point. Finally, the gathering operator \(\mathcal{G}_{\eta}^{-1}\) maps the atomic energy back to the atomic positions \(\left\{\boldsymbol{r}_{i}\right\}_{i=1}^{N}\), summing contributions across all layers. It is worth noting that the gridding, scaling, and gathering operators can be applied species-wise in practical implementations. The entire process of the LR convolution layer is summarized as
\[
E^{L R, i}=\mathcal{G}_{\eta}^{-1} \mathcal{F}^{-1} \mathcal{S}_{\eta} \mathcal{F} \mathcal{G}_{\eta}\left[\left\{\boldsymbol{q}_{i, \eta}\right\}_{\eta=1}^{P}\right]
\]
with a schematic illustration provided in the lower half of Figure 1. The detailed method is provided in Section S3 of 29, where we leverage the FINUFFT package 61 to accelerate these operators via NUFFTs. As a result, each operator in Eq. (A1) can be executed in a complexity of either \(O\left(N+N_{\mathrm{FFT}} \log N_{\mathrm{FFT}}\right)\), where \(N_{\mathrm{FFT}}\) represents the average number of grid points per layer. This highlights a key advantage of the SOG-Net: after the learning process is complete, the model inherently supports acceleration through a fast algorithm, thereby enhancing the simulation process.

