\section*{Supplemental Material}

\section*{S1: Methods for the long-range network}

In this section, we discuss the motivation and technical details of the long-range (LR) convolution layer used in our sum-of-Gaussians neural network (SOG-Net), including the derivation of the LR model, the scheme for the parameter selection of Gaussian multipliers, and the implementation details of the trained model.

\section*{1. Limitations on using the Ewald summation in MLIPs}

Our primary motivation for developing the SOG-Net model is to enhance the widely used Ewald summation framework in machine learning interatomic potentials (MLIPs). In this subsection, we first discuss why the Ewald summation framework is unsuitable for LR tails that do not follow the \(1 / r^{p}\) form. We then introduce our improved model to address these limitations.

In classical models, charged particles are often modeled as point charges, with pairwise interactions following the empirical Coulomb form \(1 / r\). Due to the long-range nature, direct computation has an unacceptably high complexity of \(O\left(N^{2}\right)\). To accelerate calculations, the Coulomb potential is often decomposed by Ewald splitting into the form
\[
\frac{1}{r}=\frac{\operatorname{erfc}(\alpha r)}{r}+\frac{\operatorname{erf}(\alpha r)}{r},
\]
where \(\operatorname{erf}(\cdot)\) and \(\operatorname{erfc}(\cdot)\) represent the error function and complementary error function, respectively, and \(\alpha\) is known as the Ewald splitting parameter. The \(\operatorname{erfc}(\alpha r) / r\) term decays rapidly in real space and can thus be truncated for efficient computation. The \(\operatorname{erf}(\alpha r) / r\) term is singular in real space but very smooth, with
\[
\frac{\operatorname{erf}(\alpha r)}{r} \rightarrow \frac{1}{r}, \quad \text { as } \quad r \rightarrow \infty \quad \text { and } \quad \alpha \in(0, \infty),
\]
making it suitable for truncation in Fourier space. Suppose that \(N\) particles, \(\left\{\boldsymbol{r}_{i}\right\}_{i=1}^{N}\), are located within a cubic box \(\Omega=[0, L]^{3}\) with weights \(\left\{q_{i}\right\}_{i=1}^{N}\). The periodic boundary conditions are imposed. The contributions on potential from the \(\operatorname{erf}(\alpha r) / r\) term is given by
\[
\Phi\left(\boldsymbol{r}_{i}\right)=\sum_{\boldsymbol{n} \in \mathbb{Z}^{3}} \sum_{j=1}^{N} \frac{q_{j} \operatorname{erf}\left(\alpha\left|\boldsymbol{r}_{i j}+L \boldsymbol{n}\right|\right)}{\left|\boldsymbol{r}_{i j}+L \boldsymbol{n}\right|}
\]
where \(\boldsymbol{r}_{i j}=\boldsymbol{r}_{i}-\boldsymbol{r}_{j}\) and the prime indicates that the case \(i=j\) and \(\boldsymbol{n}=0\) is excluded from the summation. Its Fourier spectral expansion can be expressed as
\[
\Phi\left(\boldsymbol{r}_{i}\right)=\frac{2 \pi}{V} \sum_{\boldsymbol{k} \neq \mathbf{0}} \frac{e^{-|\boldsymbol{k}|^{2} /\left(4 \alpha^{2}\right)}}{|\boldsymbol{k}|^{2}}|\rho(\boldsymbol{k})|^{2}
\]
where \(\boldsymbol{k}=2 \pi \boldsymbol{m} / L, \boldsymbol{m} \in \mathbb{Z}^{3}, \rho(\boldsymbol{k}):=\sum_{j=1}^{N} q_{j} e^{i \boldsymbol{k} \cdot \boldsymbol{r}_{j}}\) denotes the charge structure factor, and \(V=L^{3}\) denotes the volume of \(\Omega\).

Unlike classical models, molecules in density functional theory (DFT) simulations are not ideal point particles. Discrepancies arise due to spurious interactions between periodic images, particularly for charged fragments, and noise in binding energies at large distances, especially for weak interactions. Consequently, the LR decay rate of chargecharge interactions can exceed the classical \(1 / r\) Coulomb potential [19]. Other LR interactions, such as dispersion and dipole-dipole interactions, may also deviate from their idealized decay rates. These characteristics are likely to be inherited by MLIPs since they are trained on DFT data.

In the field of MLIP, Ewald summation is a commonly used approach for incorporating LR interactions. Two primary strategies have been developed: the first involves learning localized Wannier centers and introducing virtual charge sites, while the second focuses on predicting partial charges for each site while maintaining a fixed number of sites 10, 13, 14. Once these "latent variables" are inferred by the neural network, the Fourier components of the Ewald summation formula, Eq. (S4), are employed to calculate the contribution of charges to the potential energy. Recent graph-based MLIP studies have also utilized the Ewald summation for modeling LR message passing 24 . However, the use of Ewald summation, as described in Eq. (S4), assumes a predefined LR decay rate of \(1 / r\), derived from the \(\operatorname{erf}(\alpha r) / r\) term in Eq. (S2). This fixed assumption may lead to systematic errors in MLIPs when the actual LR decay rate deviates from \(1 / r\).

\section*{2. Derivation of long-range model}

To address the limitations of existing MLIPs, we replace the Ewald summation with a multilevel LR convolution layer, where the kernel function of each level is learned through a fully connected neural network, enabling adaptive learning of the LR tail while maintaining computational efficiency.

Consider the LR atomic energy in the form of
\[
E^{L R, i}=\sum_{\eta=1}^{P} q_{i, \eta} \sum_{\boldsymbol{n} \in \mathbb{Z}^{d}} \sum_{j=1}^{N}{ }^{\prime} q_{j, \eta} g_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}}\left(\boldsymbol{r}_{i j}+L \boldsymbol{n}\right)
\]
where \(P\) is the total number of layers, \(\left\{q_{i, \eta} \mid i=1, \cdots, N\right\}\) are the latent variables of \(\eta\) th layer, and \(g_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}}(\cdot)\) is a kernel function parameterized by \(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}\) and trained via a neural network. The detailed representation of \(g_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}}(\cdot)\) is provided in subsection S13. The LR energy is defined as \(E^{L R}=\frac{1}{2} \sum_{i=1}^{N} E^{L R, i}\), where the factor \(1 / 2\) account for double-counting of interaction energy. In this context, \(g_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}}\) and \(q_{i, \eta}\) at different layers can be interpreted as representations of various LR interactions and their corresponding coefficients, respectively. For \(P=1, q_{i, 1}\) is analogous to environment-dependent partial charges on individual atoms. For \(P>1\), the model can incorporate more general LR interactions, thereby enhancing the representation and expressiveness of LR effects in the MLIP.

Let us define the Fourier transform pair as
\[
\widehat{f}(\boldsymbol{k})=\int_{\Omega} f(\boldsymbol{r}) e^{-i \boldsymbol{k} \cdot \boldsymbol{r}} d \boldsymbol{r}, \quad \text { and } \quad f(\boldsymbol{r})=\frac{1}{V} \sum_{\boldsymbol{k}} \widehat{f}(\boldsymbol{k}) e^{i \boldsymbol{k} \cdot \boldsymbol{r}}
\]

For a given function \(f\) defined on \(\mathbb{R}^{3}\), the Poisson summation formula states that
\[
\sum_{\boldsymbol{n} \in \mathbb{Z}^{d}} f(\boldsymbol{r}+L \boldsymbol{n})=\frac{1}{V} \sum_{\boldsymbol{k}} \widehat{f}(\boldsymbol{k}) e^{i \boldsymbol{k} \cdot \boldsymbol{r}}
\]

By substituting the Poission summation formula into Eq. (S5), we derive the Fourier spectral \(\operatorname{expansion}\) of \(E^{L R, i}\) :
\[
E^{L R, i}=\frac{1}{V} \sum_{\eta=1}^{P} q_{i, \eta} \sum_{j=1}^{N} q_{j, \eta} \sum_{\boldsymbol{k}} \widehat{g}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}}(\boldsymbol{k}) e^{i \boldsymbol{k} \cdot\left(\boldsymbol{r}_{i}-\boldsymbol{r}_{j}\right)}-E^{\mathrm{self}, i},
\]
where
\[
E^{\mathrm{self}, i}:=\frac{1}{V} \sum_{\eta=1}^{P}\left|q_{i, \eta}\right|^{2} \sum_{\boldsymbol{k}} \widehat{g}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}}(\boldsymbol{k})
\]
denotes the unwanted self-interaction energy.
Given the slowly varying nature of LR interactions, we model \(g_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}}(\boldsymbol{r})\) as a radially symmetric function, i.e., \(g_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}}(\boldsymbol{r})= g_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}}(r)\) with \(r=|\boldsymbol{r}|\). Consequently, its Fourier transform, \(\widehat{g}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}}(\boldsymbol{k})=\widehat{g}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}}(k)\), is also radially symmetric. We refer to \(\widehat{g}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}}(k)\) as the "SOG multiplier", with the parameter \(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}\) being trainable. Our goal is to develop an efficient and scalable descriptor for the LR component of MLIPs through the following two steps: (1) The short-range (SR) descriptor is fed into a fully connected neural network to generate the latent variables \(\left\{q_{i, \eta}\right\}_{i=1}^{N}\) for each layer \(\eta=1, \cdots, P\). (2) These latent variables are substituted into Eq. (S8) to compute \(\Phi\left(\boldsymbol{r}_{i}\right)\), performed using Fourier diagonal scaling with the multiplier \(\widehat{g}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}}(k)\). While directly evaluating \(\Phi\left(\boldsymbol{r}_{i}\right)\) for all \(i=1, \ldots, N\) requires \(O\left(N N_{\text {tot }}\right)\) operations with \(N_{\text {tot }}\) the total number of Fourier grids, the LR convolution layer (detailed in Section S3) reduces this computation to near-linear complexity in both \(N\) and \(N_{\text {tot }}\).

\section*{3. Parameterization of the SOG multiplier}

In this work, we parameterize the SOG multiplier \(\widehat{g}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}}\) as
\[
\widehat{g}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}}(\boldsymbol{k})=\sum_{\ell=1}^{M} w_{\ell, \eta} e^{-k^{2} / s_{\ell, \eta}^{2}}
\]
where \(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}=\left\{w_{\ell, \eta}, s_{\ell, \eta}\right\}_{\ell=1}^{M}\) is the set of trainable parameters of the \(\eta\) th layer. We initialize the weights \(\left\{w_{\ell, \eta}\right\}\) as matrices of all ones. The bandwidths are initialized as
\[
s_{\ell, \eta}=e^{b_{\min }^{\eta}+\ell\left(b_{\max }^{\eta}-b_{\min }^{\eta}\right) / M}, \quad \ell=1, \cdots, M
\]
where \(b_{\text {min }}^{\eta}, b_{\text {max }}^{\eta} \in \mathbb{R}, b_{\text {min }}^{\eta}<b_{\text {max }}^{\eta}\) so that \(s_{\ell, \eta}\) are logarithmically equally spaced points lying on \(\left[e^{b_{\text {min }}^{\eta}}, e^{b_{\text {max }}^{\eta}}\right]\) for each \(\eta\). Here, we use logarithmic spacing nodes instead of uniform spacing to capture the multiscale characteristics of LR potentials and reduce the number of Gaussians needed for representation. The periodicity leads to a simple rule to set \(b_{\text {min }}^{\eta}\) apriori for a given error tolerance \(\varepsilon>0\) :
\[
b_{\min }^{\eta} \geq \log (2 \pi)-\log L-\log \sqrt{\log \varepsilon^{-1}}
\]
where this formula ensures that the Gaussian with the smallest bandwidth in the SOG multiplier has its support almost exclusively at the zero mode in Fourier space. Assume that a cutoff radius \(r_{c}\) is specified for the local environment while constructing the SR descriptor, we also have a simple formula to set \(b_{\max }^{\eta}\) :
\[
b_{\max }^{\eta} \leq \log \left(2 r_{c}^{-1}\right)+\log \log \varepsilon^{-1}
\]
so that \(g_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\ell}}(\boldsymbol{r}) \sim \varepsilon\) when \(r=r_{c}\). This ensure that the Gaussian with the largest bandwidth in \(g_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\ell}}\) has its support larger than \(r_{c}\) in real space.

The advantages of using an SOG to represent the SOG multiplier are threefold. First, as a type of radial basis function, Gaussians are well-suited for approximating unknown symmetric and smooth LR potentials. Second, Gaussians are renowned for minimizing the Heisenberg uncertainty principle for \(L^{2}\) functions 30, achieving an optimal trade-off between spatial and frequency localization. Given that LR potentials frequently exhibit multi-scale characteristics, meaning they have significant support in both real and Fourier spaces, the properties of Gaussian functions make them particularly well-suited for fitting such functions.

The multiplier in Eq. S10 is highly versatile and enables the imposition of specific constraints on the learned potential. In our construction, both the multiplier \(\widehat{g}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}}\) and its inverse Fourier transform \(g_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}}\) are radially symmetric, inherently ensuring translation, inversion, and rotational invariance. Additional constraints can be imposed on the coefficients \(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}\) by either solving an equation for some certain coefficients after training the others or projecting the learned LR atomic energy \(E^{L R, i}\) onto a set of symmetric basis functions defined within the local environment of each atom similar to some previous works [19, 31.

In comparison to the Gaussian Approximation Potential (GAP) 8, introduced in early machine learning models, our work presents two key distinctions. First, GAP is primarily designed to fit SR interactions, while our SOG-Net is employed to learn LR interactions in Fourier space. Second, GAP determines the coefficients through Gaussian regression, whereas our method learns \(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}\) via a neural network.

\section*{4. Model Reduction}

In practice, a key problem is how to determine the number of Gaussians \(M\) in the SOG multiplier. At the start of training, we choose a large \(M\) to ensure sufficient resolution. Following each training round, model reduction (MR) techniques 32 are used to find the optimal number of Gaussians. The MR process starts by converting the multiplier of each layer into a sum of exponentials via the change of variable \(K=k^{2}\) and applying the Laplace transform \(\mathcal{L}[\cdot]\), leading to a sum-of-poles representation:
\[
\mathscr{L}\left[\widehat{g}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}}(K)\right]=\mathscr{L}\left[\sum_{\ell=1}^{M} w_{\ell, \eta} e^{-K / s_{\ell, \eta}^{2}}\right]=\sum_{\ell=1}^{M} \frac{w_{\ell, \eta}}{z+1 / s_{\ell, \eta}^{2}}=\boldsymbol{c}(z \boldsymbol{I}-A)^{-1} \boldsymbol{b},
\]
where \(\boldsymbol{A}\) is an \((M+1) \times(M+1)\) diagonal matrix, \(\boldsymbol{b}\) and \(\boldsymbol{c}\) are column and row vectors of dimension \(M+1\), respectively. Next, one regards the rightmost term in Eq. (S14) as a transfer function of a linear dynamical system, enabling the use of the balanced truncation method to reduce the number of terms to \(\widetilde{M}<M+1\). The initial number of Gaussians used before reduction is reported in the main text. For DFT datasets, we typically start with \(M=12\) (and \(M=6\) for the toy NaCl model). After reduction, \(M\) is usually halved without loss of accuracy. The Python implementation of the reduction procedure is included in the released SOG-Net package (https://github.com/DuktigYajie/SOG-Net).

\section*{S2: Methods for the short-range network}

In this section, we discuss the construction of SR component of our SOG-Net as well as some implementation details which is useful for improve the efficiency.

The first step in our model is to generate a robust SR descriptor that preserves symmetry. This descriptor is then fed into two networks: the first, referred to as the "fitting network", generates the SR potential; the second, called the "latent network", learns the latent variables \(\left\{q_{i, \eta}\right\}\) from the SR descriptor. Notably, SOG-Net can be seamlessly integrated with any existing methods for constructing SR descriptors, such as tensor operations in Deep Potential 3 and moment tensor networks [50, or basis projections used in MACE 23, CACE 49, and LODE [20. Unless otherwise noted, the SR descriptors used in the examples presented in this paper are based on tensor operations similar to those in Deep Potential, due to their simplicity of implementation. Exploring alternative SR descriptors and identifying the most suitable approach will be a focus of future work.

Next, we briefly review the construction of the SR model. The SR component of energy, \(E^{S R}\), is computed as the sum of atomic contributions from all atoms:
\[
E^{S R}=\sum_{i=1}^{N} E^{S R, i}=\sum_{i=1}^{N} f_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{f i t}}\left(\boldsymbol{D}_{i}\right),
\]
where \(f_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{f i t}}\) is a residual neural network (ResNet), referred to as the "fitting network", and \(\boldsymbol{D}_{i}\) is a column vector that represents the local environment of atom \(i\). The vector \(\boldsymbol{D}_{i}\) is core-shelly structured as \(\boldsymbol{D}_{i}=\left\{\boldsymbol{D}_{i, c}, \boldsymbol{D}_{i, s}\right\}\), where \(\boldsymbol{D}_{i, c}\) and \(\boldsymbol{D}_{i, s}\) contain information about neighboring atoms within specified cutoff radii \(r_{c}\) and \(r_{s}\), respectively, with \(r_{c}<=r_{s}\). The details for constructing \(\boldsymbol{D}_{i, c}\) and \(\boldsymbol{D}_{i, s}\) are provided below. Please note that we may use only \(\boldsymbol{D}_{i, c}\) or \(\boldsymbol{D}_{i, s}\) in numerical tests, depending on the actual situation, as set up in Section S4. The SR component of force, \(\boldsymbol{F}^{\mathrm{SR}, i}\), can be derived by applying the automatic differentiation on \(E^{S R}\).

The core component of the SR descriptor, \(\boldsymbol{D}_{i, c}\), is derived from the environment matrix \(\boldsymbol{\mathcal { R }}_{i, c}\) and embedding matrix \(\boldsymbol{G}_{i, c}\) :
\[
\boldsymbol{D}_{i, c}=\frac{1}{N_{c}^{2}}\left(\boldsymbol{G}_{i, c}\right)^{T} \boldsymbol{\mathcal { R }}_{i, c}\left(\boldsymbol{\mathcal { R }}_{i, c}\right)^{T} \boldsymbol{G}_{i, c}^{<}
\]
where \(N_{c}\) denotes the maximum number of neighbors within the cutoff radius \(r_{c}, \boldsymbol{G}_{i, c}\) and \(\boldsymbol{\mathcal { R }}_{i, c}\) have dimensions \(N_{c} \times M_{c}\) and \(N_{c} \times 4\), respectively. Each row in \(\boldsymbol{\mathcal { R }}_{i, c}\) is a four-dimensional vector:
\[
\mathcal{R}_{i, c}=\left\{\left.\operatorname{soft}\left(r_{i j}\right) \times\left[\frac{1}{r_{i j}}, \frac{x_{i j}}{r_{i j}^{2}}, \frac{y_{i j}}{r_{i j}^{2}}, \frac{z_{i j}}{r_{i j}^{2}}\right] \right\rvert\, j=1, \cdots, N_{c}\right\},
\]
where \(r_{i j}=\left|\boldsymbol{r}_{i j}\right|\) with \(\boldsymbol{r}_{i j}:=\boldsymbol{r}_{i}-\boldsymbol{r}_{j}\). Here, the soft function soft( ⋅ )is defined by
\[
\operatorname{soft}(r)= \begin{cases}1, & r<j_{c} r_{c} \\ -6\left(\frac{r-j_{c} r_{c}}{r_{c}-j_{c} r_{c}}\right)^{5}+15\left(\frac{r-j_{c} r_{c}}{r_{c}-j_{c} r_{c}}\right)^{4}-10\left(\frac{r-j_{c} r_{c}}{r_{c}-j_{c} r_{c}}\right)^{3}+1, & j_{c} r_{c} \leq r<r_{c} \\ 0, & r \geq r_{c}\end{cases}
\]
where \(0<j_{c}<1\) is a scaling factor determining the smooth transition region. The function soft( \(r\) ) smoothly decays from 1 to 0 within the cutoff range \(r_{c}\), ensuring that its second derivative is continuous. This property guarantees continuity in the computed forces, which significantly enhances the long-term stability of MD simulations. The embedding matrix \(\boldsymbol{G}_{i, c}\) is derived from the output of a neural network
\[
\boldsymbol{G}_{i, c}=f_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{e m b, c}}\left(\left\{\left.\frac{\operatorname{soft}\left(R_{i j}\right)}{\left|R_{i j}\right|} \right\rvert\, j=1, \cdots, N_{c}\right\}\right),
\]
where the fully connected embedding neural network \(f_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{e m b, c}}\) maps soft \(\left(R_{i j}\right) / R_{i j}\) to an \(M_{c}\) dimensional vector. A submatrix of \(\boldsymbol{G}_{i, c}\) that includes its first \(M_{c}^{<}\)columns, with \(M_{c}^{<}<M_{c}\), is denoted by \(\boldsymbol{G}_{i, c}^{<}\). The resulting core component of the SR descriptor from Eq. (S2), \(\boldsymbol{D}_{i, c}\), has dimension \(M_{c} \times M_{c}^{<}\), encapsulates both angular and radial information, and preserves symmetry.

The shell component of the SR descriptor, \(\boldsymbol{D}_{i, s}\), is defined as:
\[
\boldsymbol{D}_{i, s}=\frac{1}{N_{s}} \sum_{j=1}^{N_{s}}\left(\boldsymbol{G}_{i, s}\right)_{j}
\]
where \(N_{s}\) is the maximum number of neighboring atoms within the cutoff \(r_{s}, \boldsymbol{G}_{i, s} \in \mathbb{R}^{N_{s} \times M_{s}}\) is an embedding matrix, and the summation aggregates the rows of \(\sum_{j}\). For fewer than \(N_{s}\) neighbors, the corresponding matrix terms are zero-padded. The embedding matrix \(\boldsymbol{G}_{i, s}\) comprises \(M_{s}\) nodes from the output layer of a neural network function \(f_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{e m b, s}}\) applied to \(\operatorname{soft}\left(R_{i j}\right)\) :
\[
\left(\boldsymbol{G}_{i, s}\right)_{j}=f_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{e m b, s}}\left(\operatorname{soft}\left(R_{i j}\right)\right),
\]
which includes only radial neighbor information. The soft function, soft( ⋅ ), is defined as in Eq. (S4). In practice, we set \(r_{c}<r_{s}\), since three-body interactions are more critical for close neighbors, while radial information alone suffices for distant neighbors.

After generating the core component \(\boldsymbol{D}_{i, c}\) and shell component \(\boldsymbol{D}_{i, s}, \boldsymbol{D}_{i, c}\) is flattened into a vector and concatenated with \(\boldsymbol{D}_{i, s}\) to form the short-range descriptor \(\boldsymbol{D}_{i}=\left\{\boldsymbol{D}_{i, c}, \boldsymbol{D}_{i, s}\right\}\). The whole process involves constructing a stencil of bins to identify potential neighbors, binning atoms, and looping through the stencil to assemble the neighbor list \(\mathcal{N}_{i}\) based on the core and shell cutoff radii \(r_{c}\) and \(r_{s}\). To improve efficiency, we employ the \(k d\)-tree algorithm, which uses hierarchical spatial decomposition to perform fast nearest-neighbor searches with linear complexity.

After generating the SR descriptor \(\boldsymbol{D}_{i}\), it is fed into two separate networks. The first is the fitting network, which computes the SR atomic energy as described in Eq. S1); the second is a fully connected "latent" network, \(f_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{l n n}}(\cdot)\), which maps the input SR descriptor to output latent variables. These latent variables are then processed by the LR convolution layer to compute the LR atomic energy. The acceleration of this process is outlined in the next section.

\section*{S3: FFT-accelerated fast long-range convolution}

In this section, we detail how the fast Fourier transform (FFT) is used to accelerate calculations for the LR convolution layer, as described in Eq. (S8), within our SOG-Net model. Although the FFT is a well-established approach, however, the non-uniform distribution of atoms in MD systems prevents the direct application of the FFT. Moreover, unlike the Ewald summation which interaction kernel is fully deterministic, our model employs the trainable kernel \(\widehat{\boldsymbol{g}}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}}\), which evolves dynamically during the training process. Below, we propose a fast method to address these challenges.

Let us start by introduce a localized, separable and periodic shape function \(W(\boldsymbol{r})\) along with its Fourier transform \(\widehat{W}(\boldsymbol{k})\) for spreading particles onto a uniform grid and collecting field values from the grid. By inserting the identity \(1 \equiv \widehat{W}(\boldsymbol{k})|\widehat{W}(\boldsymbol{k})|^{-2} \widehat{W}(\boldsymbol{k})\) into Eq. (S8), one has
\[
E^{L R, i}=\frac{1}{V} \sum_{\eta=1}^{P} q_{i, \eta} \sum_{\boldsymbol{k}} \widehat{W}(\boldsymbol{k}) e^{i \boldsymbol{k} \cdot \boldsymbol{r}} \frac{\widehat{g}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}}(\boldsymbol{k})}{|\widehat{W}(\boldsymbol{k})|^{2}} \sum_{j=1}^{N} q_{j, \eta} \widehat{W}(\boldsymbol{k}) e^{-i \boldsymbol{k} \cdot \boldsymbol{r}_{j}}
\]

We will use the last \(\widehat{W}(\boldsymbol{k})\) for the gridding step, the first \(\widehat{W}(\boldsymbol{k})\) for the gathering step, and \(|\widehat{W}(\boldsymbol{k})|^{-2}\) for the diagonal scaling step, respectively. The details are provided as follows.

The calculation of Eq. (S1) is performed level-by-level. For each level \(\eta\), let us define
\[
\widehat{\Phi}_{\mathrm{grid}, \eta}(\boldsymbol{k}):=\sum_{j=1}^{N} q_{j, \eta} \widehat{W}(\boldsymbol{k}) e^{-i \boldsymbol{k} \cdot \boldsymbol{r}_{j}}
\]
which is the Fourier transform of
\[
\Phi_{\mathrm{grid}, \eta}(\boldsymbol{r})=\sum_{j=1}^{N} q_{j, \eta} W\left(\boldsymbol{r}-\boldsymbol{r}_{j}\right)
\]
by the convolution theorem. The evaluating of \(\Phi_{\text {grid }, \eta}\) on a uniform grid will be defined as the gridding operator \(\mathcal{G}_{\eta}\). After this step is done, we obtain \(\widehat{\Phi}_{\text {grid }, \eta}\) from \(\Phi_{\text {grid }, \eta}\) by applying a 3D FFT operator \(\mathcal{F}\). Next, we compute
\[
\widehat{\Phi}_{\mathrm{scal}, \eta}(\boldsymbol{k}):=\frac{\widehat{g}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}}(\boldsymbol{k})}{|\widehat{W}(\boldsymbol{k})|^{2}} \widehat{\Phi}_{\mathrm{grid}, \eta}(\boldsymbol{k})
\]
for each Fourier mode which is defined as the diagonal scaling operator \(\mathcal{S}_{\eta}\) (here diagonal means without calculations for cross terms). Next, we obtain \(\Phi_{\text {scal }, \eta}\) on the real-space grid from \(\widehat{\Phi}_{\text {scal }, \eta}\) by applying an 3D inverse FFT (IFFT) operator \(\mathcal{F}^{-1}\). Note that Eq. (S4) allow us to write Eq. (S1) as
\[
E^{L R, i}=\sum_{\eta=1}^{P} q_{i, \eta} \int_{\Omega} \Phi_{\mathrm{scal}, \eta}\left(\boldsymbol{r}^{\prime}\right) W\left(\boldsymbol{r}_{i}-\boldsymbol{r}^{\prime}\right) d \boldsymbol{r}^{\prime}:=\sum_{\eta=1}^{P} q_{i, \eta} \Phi_{i, \eta}
\]
by applying again the convolution theorem. The gathering operator \(\mathcal{G}_{\eta}^{-1}\) is performed by discretizing Eq. (S5) on a uniform grid using the trapezoidal rule and summing the contributions from grids for each atom. When the shape function \(W\) is smooth and compactly supported, the algorithm achieves spectral convergence as the Fourier grid size increases due to the use of FFT. The entire process of the LR convolution layer is summarized as:
\[
E^{L R, i}=\sum_{\eta=1}^{P} q_{i, \eta} \mathcal{G}_{\eta}^{-1} \mathcal{F}^{-1} \mathcal{S}_{\eta} \mathcal{F} \mathcal{G}_{\eta}\left[\left\{q_{j, \eta} \mid j=1, \cdots, N ; \eta=1, \cdots, P\right\}\right]
\]

It is worth noting that the gridding, scaling, and gathering operators can be applied species-wise for multi-species systems. Algorithm 1 outlines the procedure for this fast algorithm, which significantly accelerates both the training and testing processes of our SOG-Net model.

In practice, these operators are efficiently implemented using existing packages. The gridding and 3D FFT operators can be performed by Type-1 non-uniform FFTs (NUFFTs) [26, 27, while the 3D IFFT and gathering operators can be performed by Type-2 NUFFTs. The NUFFT is particularly effective for accelerating the exponential sums of the form \(\sum_{i} w_{i} e^{i \boldsymbol{k} \cdot \boldsymbol{r}_{i}}\), where \(\boldsymbol{k}\) and/or \(\boldsymbol{r}_{i}\) exhibit non-uniform distributions. In this work, we utilize the FINUFFT package 61 to accelerate the NUFFT computations. The error tolerance is set to \(10^{-4}\), and the so-called "exponential of semicircle" kernel is used as the shape function. The total computational complexity for each layer \(\eta\) is \(O\left(N+N_{\mathrm{FFT}, \eta} \log N_{\mathrm{FFT}, \eta}\right)\), where \(N_{\mathrm{FFT}, \eta}\) denotes the size of the Fourier grid for layer \(\eta\).
```
Algorithm 1 Accelerating the long-range convolution layer in the SOG-Net model
    Input: Positions \(\left\{\boldsymbol{r}_{i}\right\}\), the side length \(L\), the shape function \(W\), and the size of FFT grid \(N_{\mathrm{FFT}, \eta}\), the latent variables
    \(\left\{q_{i, \eta}\right\}\) and the SOG multipliers \(\widehat{g}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}}\) at each layer \(\eta=1, \cdots, P\).
    (Gridding) For each layer \(\eta\), spreading latent variables from atomic locations to a uniform grid to obtain \(\Phi_{\text {grid }, \eta}\) by using
    Eq. (S3).
    (FFT) Obtain \(\widehat{\Phi}_{\text {grid }, \eta}\) on the corresponding Fourier grid from \(\Phi_{\text {grid }, \eta}\) via a 3D FFT.
    (Diagonal Scaling) For each layer \(\eta\), obtain \(\widehat{\Phi}_{\text {scal }, \eta}\) by scaling \(\widehat{\Phi}_{\text {grid }, \eta}\) with the SOG multiplier \(\widehat{g}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}}\) and the \(\widehat{W}^{-2}\) term as in
    Eq. (S4).
    (IFFT) Obtain \(\Phi_{\text {scal }, \eta}\) on real-space grids from \(\widehat{\Phi}_{\text {scal }, \eta}\) via a 3D IFFT.
    (Gathering) For each layer \(\eta\), obtain \(\Phi_{i, \eta}\) by gathering contributions from grids as in Eq. S5.
    Output: Long-range atomic energy \(E^{L R, i}=\sum_{\eta=1}^{P} q_{i, \eta} \Phi_{i, \eta}\).
```


The LR component of the force, \(\boldsymbol{F}^{L R, i}\), can be derived by applying automatic differentiation to \(E^{L R}\). However, applying automatic differentiation for FFT and IFFT operators in the current implementation of the FINUFFT package is of rather expensive. We instead compute the gradient of \(E^{L R}\) with respect to the atomic position \(\boldsymbol{r}_{i}\) directly using Eq. (S5). The formula is expressed as:
\[
\boldsymbol{F}^{L R, i}=-\nabla_{\boldsymbol{r}_{i}} E^{L R}=\frac{1}{2 V} \sum_{\eta=1}^{P} q_{i, \eta} \sum_{\boldsymbol{k}} \boldsymbol{k} \widehat{g}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\eta}}(\boldsymbol{k}) \operatorname{Im}\left[e^{i \boldsymbol{k} \cdot \boldsymbol{r}_{i}} \sum_{j=1}^{N} q_{j, \eta} e^{-i \boldsymbol{k} \cdot \boldsymbol{r}_{j}}\right],
\]
where \(\operatorname{Im}[\cdot]\) denotes the imaginary part. In practice, the computation of Eq. S7) can be also accelerated by utilizing three additional Type-2 NUFFTs during the gathering step.

\section*{S4: Training and simulation details}

In this section, we provide details on the training and simulation setups used in our tests.

We start by describing the loss function and the error metrics used in our analysis. Given the importance of forces compared to energy in MD simulations, the loss function is defined as the mean squared error (MSE) of forces:
\[
\mathcal{E}_{\text {loss }}^{\text {force }}:=\frac{1}{N_{\text {batch }}} \sum_{n=1}^{N_{\text {batch }}} \sum_{i=1}^{N}\left\|\boldsymbol{F}_{\text {tot }, n}^{\text {SOG-Net }}\left(\boldsymbol{r}_{i}\right)-\boldsymbol{F}_{\text {tot }, n}^{\text {exact }}\left(\boldsymbol{r}_{i}\right)\right\|^{2},
\]
where \(n\) indexes the batches sampled from the training set in each epoch, \(N_{\text {batch }}\) is the total number of batches, and \(\boldsymbol{F}_{\text {tot }, n}^{\text {SOG-Net }}\) and \(\boldsymbol{F}_{\text {tot }, n}^{\text {exact }}\) represent the forces calculated by SOG-Net and the reference solution derived from DFT, respectively. For tests involving binding curves of dimers, the loss function also involves the MSE of energies:
\[
\mathcal{E}_{\text {loss }}^{\text {energy }}:=\frac{1}{N_{\text {batch }}} \sum_{n=1}^{N_{\text {batch }}}\left\|E_{\text {tot }, n}^{\text {SOG-Net }}-E_{\text {tot }, n}^{\text {exact }}\right\|^{2}
\]

The loss function is given by \(\mathcal{E}_{\text {loss }}=\mathcal{E}_{\text {loss }}^{\text {energy }}+\beta \mathcal{E}_{\text {loss }}^{\text {force }}\) where \(\beta\) is a non-negative factor. For dimer pairs, we set \(\beta=1000\). More implementation details can be found in our code repository (https://github.com/DuktigYajie/SOG-Net).

During testing, the relative root-mean-square error (R-RMSE) of forces is used as the error metric for the NaCl electrolyte model. It is defined as:
\[
\mathcal{E}_{\text {R-RMSE }}:=\sqrt{\frac{\sum_{m=1}^{N_{\text {test }}} \sum_{i=1}^{N}\left\|\boldsymbol{F}_{\text {tot }, m}^{\text {exact }}\left(\boldsymbol{r}_{i}\right)-\boldsymbol{F}_{\text {tot }, m}^{\text {SOG-Net }}\left(\boldsymbol{r}_{i}\right)\right\|^{2}}{\sum_{m=1}^{N_{\text {test }}} \sum_{i=1}^{N}\left\|\boldsymbol{F}_{\text {tot }, m}^{\text {exact }}\left(\boldsymbol{r}_{i}\right)\right\|^{2}}},
\]
where \(m\) indexes the test configurations, and \(N_{\text {test }}\) is the size of testing set. For tests involving binding energy curves, the RMSE of energies is also employed as an error metric. In other tests, we calculate the RMSE of forces across all test configurations as:
\[
\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{RMSE}}^{\text {force }}:=\sqrt{\frac{1}{\mathrm{NN}_{\mathrm{test}}} \sum_{m=1}^{N_{\mathrm{test}}} \sum_{i=1}^{N}\left\|\boldsymbol{F}_{\mathrm{tot}, m}^{\text {exact }}\left(\boldsymbol{r}_{i}\right)-\boldsymbol{F}_{\mathrm{tot}, m}^{\mathrm{SOG}-\mathrm{Net}}\left(\boldsymbol{r}_{i}\right)\right\|^{2}} .
\]

We next outline the network architecture and training approaches used in our tests. For the NaCl electrolyte model tests, we use only the shell component of the SR network described in Section S2, with cutoff radii set to \(r_{c}=0\) and \(r_{s}=2\) (adjusted as specified, if applicable). The sizes of the embedding network \(f_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text {emb, }}}\) and the fitting network \(f_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text {fit }}}\) are configured as \((2,4,8,16,32)\) and \((32,32,32,32,32)\), respectively. The activation function after each layer is set as tanh to prevent gradient disappearance/explosion. In this system, all ions are modeled as soft spheres of diameter \(\sigma\) with \(\sigma\) the distance unit, that interact through a force field that includes both the Coulomb potential \(1 / r\) and the dispersive Lennard-Jones (LJ) potential. Since the charge strengths are explicitly defined for these tests, with Na and Cl atoms assigned +1 and -1 charges, respectively, the latent network is not required to generate latent variables from the SR descriptor. Parameters for the LR component of SOG-Net model are detailed in the main text. The models are trained using the Adam optimizer with an initial learning rate of 0.001 , which decays by a factor of 0.97 every 10 epochs. The training process comprises four stages: starting with a batch size of 8 configurations and 200 epochs, with both the batch size and the number of epochs doubling at each subsequent stage.

For each classes of molecular pair in the dimer tests, we employ a hybrid SR descriptor, including both \(\boldsymbol{D}_{i, c}\) and \(\boldsymbol{D}_{i, s}\), in our SOG-Net model. The cutoff radii and scaling factors are set to \(10 \AA\) and \(3 / 4\) for the both components. In our model, the sizes of the embedding networks \(f_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{e m b, c}}\) and \(f_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{e m b, s}}\) are configured as ( \(20,40,80\) ), and the parameter \(M_{c}^{<}\)for constructing \(\boldsymbol{D}_{i, c}\) is set as 80 . The sizes of the fitting network \(f_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{f i t}}\) and the latent network \(f_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{l n n}}\) are set to \((64,64,64)\) and ( \(20,40,80,1\) ), respectively. Consequently, a single latent variable layer ( \(P=1\) ) is sufficient for this set of tests. All networks uses the tanh activation function after each layer. Parameters for the LR component of the SOG-Net model are detailed in the main text. The models are trained using the Adam optimizer with an initial learning rate of 0.001 , which decays by a factor of 0.98 every 10 epochs. The training process comprises of three stages: starting with a batch size of 2 configurations and 800 epochs, with both the batch size and the number of epochs doubling at each subsequent stage. Note that we compare SOG-Net with the latent Ewald summation (LES) method 36 in the main text. The LES results, taken from the original paper, were obtained using a CACE 49 SR descriptor with a cutoff of \(r_{c}=5 \AA\) and one message-passing layer, giving an effective receptive field of approximately \(10 \AA\). Accordingly, both the CACE and DeepMD descriptors used in our tests capture a similar local environment.

These comparisons are intended to demonstrate SOG-Net's ability to capture diverse LR decay behaviors, though the results may also reflect differences in training protocols across works.

For the liquid water tests, we again use the core-shell structured SR descriptor in our SOG-Net model, with cutoff radii set to \(4 \AA\) and \(7 \AA\) for the core and shell components, respectively. The dimensions of embedding matrices and fitting matrix are set to \((25,50,100)\) and \((120,120,120)\), respectively. The scaling factors \(j_{c}\) and \(j_{s}\) used in the core and shell components are set to \(1 / 8\) and \(1 / 14\). The parameter \(M_{c}^{<}\)for the core component is set as 16 . The sizes of the latent network are configured as \((25,50,100,16)\), so that the number of layer of latent variables is set as 16 . All networks uses the tanh activation function after each layer. Parameters for the LR component of the SOG-Net model are detailed in the main text. The models are trained using the Adam optimizer with an initial learning rate of 0.001 , which decays by a factor of 0.997 every 10 epochs. The training process comprises of two stages: starting with a batch size of 50 configurations and 10000 epochs, with both the batch size and the number of epochs doubling at the subsequent stage. The MD simulations are performed in the ASE package, and the LAMMPS interface implementation is our future work. To compute the charge structure factors, we are assuming that hydrogen atoms and oxygen atoms have charges of \(+0.4238 e\) and \(-0.8476 e\), respectively, similar to previous works 36 . This assumption only affects the absolute amplitude of the correlation function in Figure 4 of the main text, without altering the relative scale.

For the \(\mathrm{C}_{10} \mathrm{H}_{2} / \mathrm{C}_{10} \mathrm{H}_{3}^{+}, \mathrm{Na}_{8 / 9} \mathrm{Cl}_{8}^{+}, \mathrm{Au}_{2}\) clusters on \(\mathrm{MgO}(001)\), polar dipeptide, and molten bulk sodium chloride tests, we use the CACE [49] SR descriptor in our SOG-Net model. Comparison results, other than the SOG-Net model, for the first four datasets and the final dataset were obtained from existing studies 36, 37, respectively. Accordingly, we set the cutoff radii to \(r_{c}=4.23,5.29,5.5,4.0\), and \(6.0 \AA\), respectively, to match the settings in these references. Other parameters of SR descriptor are set to the same as in 37: For \(\mathrm{C}_{10} \mathrm{H}_{2} / \mathrm{C}_{10} \mathrm{H}_{3}^{+}\), we use 6 Bessel radial functions, \(c=8, l_{\text {max }}=3, \nu_{\text {max }}=3, N_{\text {embedding }}=2\), and no message passing. For \(\mathrm{Na}_{8 / 9} \mathrm{Cl}_{8}^{+}\), we use 6 Bessel radial functions, \(c=8, l_{\max }=3, \nu_{\max }=3, N_{\text {embedding }}=2\), and no message passing. For Au2 clusters on \(\operatorname{MgO}(001)\), we use 6 Bessel radial functions, \(c=12, l_{\max }=3, \nu_{\max }=3, N_{\text {embedding }}=4\), and no message passing. For polar dipeptide tests, we use 6 trainable Bessel radial functions, \(c=12, l_{\max }=4, \nu_{\max }=3\), one message passing layer, and different embeddings of sender and receiver nodes with \(N_{\text {embedding }}=4\). For SOG-Net, the LR energy from Eq. (S8) is computed in real space as the configurations are with aperiodic conditions. The latent variables ( \(P=1\) ) are interpreted as atomic SOG-Net charges and used to compute dipole and quadrupole moments. To ensure translational invariance in quadrupole comparison, we subtract the trace from both the predicted and DFT quadrupole moments. The molten sodium chloride dataset contains 1014 structures ( \(80 \%\) train and \(20 \%\) validation) of 64 Na and 64 Cl atoms. The configurations are taken from VASP MD simulations with 50000 steps and a time step of 1.5 fs . The PAW potentials used were "PAW_PBE Na_pv 19Sep2006" for Na and "PAW_PBE Cl 06Sep2006", for Cl, considering seven valence electrons for each element. We use 6 Bessel radial functions, \(c=12, l_{\max }=3, \nu_{\max }=3, N_{\text {embedding }}=3\), and zero or one message passing layer. For the SOG-Net, we use a 4-layer latent variable, \(M=12\) Gaussians, and 31 Fourier modes along each direction.

